.SH NAME
hed\-multicursor \- Extra cursors with synchronized edits.
Add extra cursors and every keystroke after that \[em] motion, insert,
operator, leader chord, mode change \[em] fires once per cursor.
Or turn sync off and hop between cursors while they hold their
positions.
.PP
Extra cursors with synchronized edits.
Add extra cursors and every keystroke after that \[em] motion, insert,
operator, leader chord, mode change \[em] fires once per cursor.
Or turn \f[B]sync\f[R] off and hop between cursors while they hold their
positions.
.SH Two modes
\f[B]Sync on (default).\f[R] Synchronized editing: every keypress runs
the full per\-mode dispatch once per cursor (see \[lq]How replay
works\[rq] below).
.PP
\f[B]Sync off\f[R] (\f[CR]:mc_sync\f[R] / \f[CR]<space>ms\f[R]).
Extra cursors are passive markers.
You move and edit with the active cursor only; the others stay where you
put them (core auto\-shifts them when edits land before them, so they
stay glued to their text).
Use \f[CR]:mc_jump_next\f[R] / \f[CR]:mc_jump_prev\f[R]
(\f[CR]<space>mj\f[R] / \f[CR]<space>mk\f[R]) to make another cursor the
active one.
.SH Per\-window cursor sets
Cursor sets belong to a \f[B](buffer, window) pair\f[R].
Two windows showing the same buffer each have their own set; switching
focus or buffers parks one set and restores the other (handled by core,
\f[CR]buf_cursors_bind_window\f[R]).
A buffer that\[cq]s no longer shown in any window keeps the set of the
last window that showed it, and the next window to show the buffer
adopts that set.
