.TE
.PP
\f[CR]<C\-n>\f[R] mirrors VSCode\[cq]s \f[CR]Ctrl\-D\f[R] (\[lq]select
next occurrence\[rq]).
On the first press it picks up the word under the cursor (or a
single\-line visual selection), seeds \f[CR]E.search_query\f[R] with it,
and puts a new cursor at the next match.
Each subsequent press adds one more cursor at the following match,
wrapping past the end of the buffer.
The new cursor lands at the same offset inside the match as the
cursor\[cq]s offset inside the original word/selection, so synced edits
line up at every cursor.
.PP
\f[CR]\[aq]*\f[R] / \f[CR]<M\-n>\f[R] mirrors VSCode\[cq]s
\f[CR]Ctrl\-Shift\-L\f[R] (\[lq]select all occurrences\[rq]): one press
puts a cursor at \f[B]every\f[R] match of the word under cursor (normal)
or the single\-line selection (visual) across the whole buffer,
replacing the current cursor set.
The active cursor stays on the occurrence the query came from.
Matches are non\-overlapping, scanned left to right.
With sync on (the default) your next edit lands at all of them at once;
use \f[CR]Q\f[R] to skip any match you don\[cq]t want.
.PP
\f[CR]Q\f[R] is \[lq]skip this match\[rq]: drop the active cursor and
advance to the next cursor in \f[CR](y, x)\f[R] order, cycling back to
the first when there\[cq]s nothing after the current one.
.SH How replay works (sync on)
Two phases.
.PP
\f[B]Phase 1\f[R] \[em] dispatch the key once at the original active
cursor with \f[CR]in_replay = 0\f[R].
Multicursor\[cq]s own commands (\f[CR]C\-n\f[R], \f[CR]Q\f[R],
\f[CR]mc_add_below\f[R], \f[CR]mc_add_above\f[R], \f[CR]mc_clear\f[R])
actually run here: they guard themselves with
\f[CR]if (in_replay) return;\f[R] so they only execute on the active
dispatch, never on a per\-cursor replay.
If this dispatch changed the cursor count (a list operation happened),
we stop \[em] exactly one dispatch is the right amount, and replaying it
at every extra would compound.
.PP
\f[B]Phase 2\f[R] \[em] replay at every cursor except the original
active, sorted descending by \f[CR](y, x)\f[R] so an edit at a later
position doesn\[cq]t shift the column of an unprocessed cursor.
The pre\-keypress mode and key\-sequence buffer are restored before each
replay, so:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]i\f[R] enters INSERT at every cursor instead of typing
\f[CR]i\f[R] literally at the second one.
.IP \[bu] 2
Multi\-key sequences (\f[CR]dd\f[R], \f[CR]ciw\f[R], \f[CR]ya\[dq]\f[R])
replay correctly \[em] the first key arms the operator at every cursor,
and the next key completes it at every cursor.
.IP \[bu] 2
Leader bindings work the same way.
.PP
A re\-entrancy guard (\f[CR]in_replay\f[R]) prevents Phase 2 dispatches
from re\-firing the keypress hook on themselves.
.SS Skipped contexts
The hook bails out (no replay, doesn\[cq]t consume the event) when sync
is off, or when a single global UI is active:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Prompt input\f[R] (\f[CR]:\f[R] / \f[CR]/\f[R] / \f[CR]?\f[R]):
replaying would type each character N times into the same prompt buffer.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Modal windows\f[R] (dired confirms, LSP popups, selectlist): these
route keys globally; per\-cursor replay has no meaning there.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Visual modes\f[R] (and the key that enters one,
\f[CR]v\f[R]/\f[CR]V\f[R]/\f[CR]C\-v\f[R]): the selection anchor is
global (one per window, not per cursor), so replaying would re\-anchor
it at every cursor and leave it wherever the last replay ran.
Selection keys act on the active cursor only; sync resumes once the
selection is gone (e.g.\ after \f[CR]c\f[R] drops you into insert, typed
characters replay at every cursor again).
.PP
The same bail\-out triggers inside Phase 2 if a Phase 1 dispatch opens a
prompt or modal mid\-sequence (e.g.\ \f[CR]:\f[R] from normal mode).
.SH Limitations
.IP \[bu] 2
Visual\-block mode interactions are minimal \[em] the plugin treats
block selection as a single primary cursor with the column span driven
by the renderer, not by per\-cursor objects.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]:mc_next_match\f[R] only supports single\-line visual selections;
multi\-line selections report \[lq]multi\-line selection not
supported\[rq].
.IP \[bu] 2
Operations that read/modify a single global selection (yank registers,
search, fzf) still happen once per replay rather than being merged.
Useful in practice; surprising in edge cases.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]deinit\f[R] is a no\-op (the editor doesn\[cq]t yet support
unregistering hooks), so toggling this plugin off requires a
\f[CR]:reload\f[R].
